{
  "description": "Typography M2 differential corpus allowlist. C-LAT, C-TH and C-MIX are set at 1 px per font unit through the legacy fontkit path (FontRegistry.setLines) and the M1 HarfBuzz path (createTypesetter, optical size at the axis default). Every line whose glyph IDs or positions (±0.5 unit) differ is listed here with its kind. `change` is computed by tests/typography-m2-differential.test.ts: index:legacy→M1 glyph names when the IDs differ, else the pair whose advance differs. Each kind is proved mechanically by that test; any new, changed or vanished difference fails `npm test`. Do not add an entry without a kind whose proof covers it.",
  "shapers": {
    "legacy": "fontkit@2.0.4",
    "m1": "harfbuzzjs@1.6.4/harfbuzz@15.0.0"
  },
  "kinds": {
    "script-itemization": {
      "explanation": "Plan §3.1 one-script-per-line defect, fixed by M1's script-runs@1.0.0 itemizer rather than by the shaper. Legacy shapes a whole mixed line with fontkit's first strong script. A Latin-first line therefore shapes its Thai under latn: Thai contextual mark forms (uni0E48.small, uni0E49.narrow, uni0E0D.less, uni0E4B.small) are not chosen, and Thai pair kerning (ใ+ช −12, เ+ศ −10) is lost. A Thai-first line shapes its Latin under thai, which loses Latin pair kerning (r+e −20 in 'Store').",
      "proof": "fontkit itself, given the same script-runs@1.0.0 runs, each shaped with its script tag, reproduces the M1 glyph IDs and positions exactly; the line has both Latin and Thai runs."
    },
    "thai-mark-shaping": {
      "explanation": "fontkit 2.0.4 has no Thai shaper. HarfBuzz's Thai shaper decomposes sara am (U+0E33) and moves the nikhahit (U+0E4D) before the tone mark, so the tone mark sits above the nikhahit and takes its .small form (น้ำ: uni0E4D uni0E49.small uni0E32). It also keeps the tone mark's left-shifted .narrow form over a tall consonant (ป ฝ ฟ) when a below vowel comes between them (ปู่, ปุ่). fontkit decomposes sara am but does not reorder it, and its contextual lookup misses the tone-mark form across the below vowel. These are fontkit defects (ADR 0019, Evidence).",
      "proof": "The line is Thai only, so itemization changes nothing, and fontkit per script run still differs from M1. Both paths have the same characters and the same non-mark glyphs in the same order, and every glyph that differs is a Thai mark."
    },
    "inter-endash-case": {
      "explanation": "Inter's own calt rule raises an en dash to endash.case between figures or capitals, including across a space. fontkit applies the rule when the dash touches its neighbours (5–2) but drops it when the context spans a space (5 – 2). HarfBuzz applies the font's rule in both cases and, as the font intends, not between lowercase letters (a – a). fontkit is the incorrect one.",
      "proof": "Only endash→endash.case differs; HarfBuzz with calt off gives the legacy glyphs; the 5–2, 5 – 2 and a – a probes behave as described on both shapers."
    },
    "dflt-duplicate-kern": {
      "explanation": "Inter's GPOS kern feature lists lookups [2, 3, 1] under latn but [2, 3, 1, 2, 3, 1] under DFLT. A line of figures has no strong script, so fontkit shapes it under DFLT and applies the 7→8 pair twice (−58 units). HarfBuzz applies each lookup once per stage, as the OpenType processing model requires (−29), and fontkit also gives −29 under latn. fontkit is the incorrect one (ADR 0019, Pre-ship conditions).",
      "proof": "The glyph IDs match; the only advance that differs is seven before eight, by exactly +29 units on M1; fontkit under an explicit latn script reproduces the M1 advances; the line itemizes as one Common run."
    }
  },
